{
  "image_name": "2f94c97563449ee2371762437b6186e4.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three innate immune signaling pathways and highlights how the E3 ligase Pellino 3 influences inflammatory and survival responses.\n\nOn the left, TLR3 signaling is triggered in an endosome by double-stranded RNA. Through TRIF, the pathway branches toward RIP1, IKKε/TBK1, TRAF6, NF-κB, IRF3, and IRF7, leading to transcription of inflammatory genes and type I interferons. Pellino 3 is shown acting around the TRAF6/ubiquitin-dependent steps, suggesting it helps promote these downstream signals.\n\nIn the center, NOD2 signaling in an intestinal epithelial cell responds to bacterial products such as MDP. NOD2 and RIP2 activate TAK1 and IKKs, feeding into MAP kinase pathways like JNK, p38, and ERK as well as NF-κB. The output is AP-1- and NF-κB-driven production of cytokines, chemokines, and bactericidal peptides. This panel emphasizes epithelial antimicrobial defense against pathogens.\n\nOn the right, TNF signaling through TNFR1 balances cell survival and cell death. RIP1, TRADD, TRAF2/5, and cIAPs support ubiquitin-dependent NF-κB activation and anti-apoptotic gene expression, whereas formation of a DISC with FADD and pro-caspase 8 drives caspase activation, RIP1 cleavage, and loss of survival functions. Pellino 3 appears to modulate RIP1-centered decisions between inflammation, survival, and apoptosis.",
